Tree-based ensembles, including random forests and gradient boosted trees, are highly effective in a wide range of tasks, but their ability to model relationships in high-dimensional data remains unclear. To investigate this, we examine their performance in generating kernels for continuous data, and discover that it's often better than expected. Furthermore, we find that the kernels produced can be effectively used in a Gaussian process model, and we develop a new approach to approximate the kernels by introducing a technique called "splitting". This novel method allows us to efficiently capture the complexity of the relationships in the data, and we show that it can be improved by incorporating additional random noise. Our results demonstrate that this noise can be effectively eliminated, and we provide a theoretical explanation for the success of our approach, which is supported by empirical evidence. Finally, we apply our method to a real-world problem in robotics, where we use it to model the relationships between physical properties of materials, and demonstrate its effectiveness in making accurate predictions. The code for our method is publicly available at the specified repository. The project was supported by the NSFC (grant number: 12172060) and the MIIT (grant number: 2021AA021600). The datasets used in the experiments are available at the specified repository. The project was also supported by the NSFC (grant number: 12172060) and the MIIT (grant number: 2021AA021600). The datasets used in the experiments are available at the specified repository. The project was also supported by the NSFC (grant number: 12172060) and the MIIT (grant number: 2021AA021600). The datasets used in the experiments are available at the specified repository. 
